       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. A single 2,400 mg dose of ibuprofen is above any recommended dose, and 9,600 mg/day is about three times the maximum daily dose. I don't know of an indication that calls for it. Doses in this range can cause GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, and CNS effects, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function. Entering it could directly harm your patient.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **If there was a typo**, I can enter 600 mg PO QID or 800 mg PO TID (2,400 mg/day) right now. I can also enter another dose within the labeled range.\n- **If you need stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect**, I'd suggest talking with pharmacy or your attending about other options, such as scheduled acetaminophen, other adjuncts, or a different agent.\n- **If there's a specific protocol behind this dose**, please run it through pharmacy and your attending. They can place or co-sign the order through the proper channels.\n\nTell me which dose you want, and I'll enter it."
      }
